# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 1.9260e+05 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 2.4000 84.719 2.9637e+06 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~85 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 Y first_yield 7.0230 55.892 4.7617e+08 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.18 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
4 F flare 7.1333 55.216 1.3145e+09 stylized  Light rises x15.9 in 0.28 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 55.9 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 54.6 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply 80% of the rise and 75% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
5 B first_breakup 7.2240 54.660 2.5290e+09 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x5.3 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
6 F flare 7.7154 51.686 1.0782e+10 stylized  Light rises x1.6 in 0.20 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: split gen 1 at 51.3 km (37% of m0). Dust groups supply 51% of the rise and 60% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
7 F flare 8.2450 48.531 2.0738e+10 stylized  Light rises x1.5 in 0.15 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 2 at 49.0 km (13% of m0); yield gen 1 at 48.8 km (12% of m0); split gen 1 at 48.2 km (12% of m0) +2 more. Dust groups supply 7% of the rise and 38% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 P peak 8.6820 46.007 2.9320e+10 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+2.3 km from the peak of energy deposition (43.8 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 17.2 km/s).
9 b bump 8.7150 45.824 2.8748e+10 stylized  Small fluctuation, 9% above the local trend; coincides with a split (gen 2) at 45.7 km. The fine structure of this phase is set by the cascade rules, so its pattern is not predictive of a real event.
10 - drag_overtakes_ablation 9.4403 42.063 1.5787e+10 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
11 D rolloff 9.7021 40.864 1.0784e+10 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 1.02 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 68% of v0, 39% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
12 T tail 12.6122 33.152 2.9273e+07 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
